THOUGHT: NullAway complains because existingLease is annotated @Nullable yet dereferenced in the debug log and return statement. Even though the code path is guarded by existingStatus != null (which implies existingLease != null), NullAway doesn’t infer that. I’ll fix this by reusing existingStatus and the instance ID instead of re-dereferencing existingLease, and by guarding any remaining use of existingLease with a null check. After editing, I’ll run the targeted Gradle compile to ensure the total NullAway error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/eureka && sed -i '32,40c\	      if ((existingStatus != null)
	          && (InstanceInfo.InstanceStatus.OUT_OF_SERVICE.equals(existingStatus)
	              || InstanceInfo.InstanceStatus.UP.equals(existingStatus))) {
	        String instanceId = instanceInfo.getId();
	        if (existingLease != null && existingLease.getHolder() != null) {
	          instanceId = existingLease.getHolder().getId();
	        }
	        logger.debug(
	            "There is already an existing lease with status {}  for instance {}",
	            existingStatus.name(),
	            instanceId);
	        return StatusOverrideResult.matchingStatus(existingStatus);
	      }' eureka-core/src/main/java/com/netflix/eureka/registry/rule/LeaseExistsRule.java && ./gradlew eureka-core:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -i "NullAway" new_build_output.log | tail -n 5
```

